course: dictionary
lesson: 8343
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. Roof has three. In «Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare», a roof is the top covering of a building. In «a special organ in the roof of the mouth», it is the hard top part inside your mouth.
explain: Look at the words around it. Walls, falls and buildings tell you the roof is on a house. Tongue, scent and mouth tell you it is inside the mouth. Then check: put your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Some meanings only make sense in a phrase. «Hit the roof» does not mean hitting anything: it means getting very angry. «A roof over your head» means a place to live.
fact: A roof is the top covering of a building or vehicle.
fact: The roof of your mouth is the hard top part inside your mouth.
fact: A roof over your head means a place to live.
fact: Sloping roofs let rain run off, and roofs can be made of tiles, slate, metal or thatch.
fact: «Hit the roof» means get very angry, and «raise the roof» means be very noisy.
exercise: In «Never jump from high walls or roofs for a dare», what is a roof? | A ladder used by builders | A place to sleep in a tent | *The top covering of a building | The hard top part inside your mouth
exercise: In «a special organ in the roof of the mouth», what does roof mean? | A pole for a phone mast | *The hard top part inside your mouth | The top covering of a building | A cover for a bike
exercise: Jo says her gran is going to hit the roof. What does that mean? | Her gran is going to climb the house | Her gran is going to be very quiet | *Her gran is going to be very angry | Her gran is going to sleep
test: «Sloping roofs let water run off.» Why do roofs slope? | To keep the birds in | To hide the chimney | *To let rain run off | To make the house look taller
test: Which phrase means being very noisy? | A roof over your head | Hit the roof | Under one roof | *Raise the roof
exam: Which sentence uses roof to mean the hard top part inside your mouth? | Toad's coach-house is piled up to the roof | A pole on a roof is a phone mast you have walked past | *Snakes smell with a special organ in the roof of the mouth | Never climb onto a roof; it is easy to fall
